AUDIO: 'Imagine getting into an aircraft that weighs less than you. This is not a drone, not a motorized paraglider, but a real electric airplane made of carbon fiber, with wings, an engine, and a battery. And yet the'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'entire plane weighed only about 70 kg. Yes , an aircraft that is lighter than a motorcycle. In fact, he is lighter than the average'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'adult passenger. It was FlyNano. Perhaps one of the most extreme attempts to create a truly personal electric aircraft. And looking at it'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'today, the interesting question may not be why FlyNano disappeared. And whether this idea deserves another chance. Note that there was another ultra-light aircraft called the E-Fan'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electric Cri-Cri, which flew across the English Channel back in 2015. But Cri-Cri weighed 80 kg, which was also impressive. However, the Fly Nano is a full 10 kg'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lighter. Fly Nano hails from Finland and was designed by Finnish aeronautical engineer Aki Suokas. The aircraft attracted attention around 2011, and'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in 2012 an electric prototype took to the air. From the very beginning, the Fly Nano was built around one extraordinary goal—70 kg. The reason is'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that in this weight category, the aircraft could be used without a pilot\'s license, at least in some countries. And it was this figure that influenced virtually every'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aspect of the aircraft. But before we look at how the entire aircraft was made so light, we need to first look at what is arguably the Fly Nano\'s most unique feature— its wings. The Fly Nano had a'

[01:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wingspan of only 4.8 m. For comparison, many light aircraft have wingspans of about twice that. Yet the Fly Nano managed to'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fit sufficient lifting surface into this tiny wingspan thanks to an unusual closed wing design. When viewed from the front, the front and rear lifting surfaces are connected at'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the ends, forming something like a box. This arrangement is related to an aerodynamic idea that dates back to Ludwig Prandtl. Prandtl showed that if'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the wingspan of an aircraft is limited, the arrangement of lifting surfaces in three dimensions can reduce the inductive drag compared to a conventional flat wing. Inductive drag is the'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aerodynamic price an aircraft pays for creating lift. Usually, if you want to make an airplane more efficient, one solution is to simply increase the wingspan. This is why high-performance'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gliders have extremely long and thin wings. But the Fly Nano couldn\'t do that. It was supposed to be tiny, measuring only 3.8 m long and 4.8 m wide. So, instead of'

[03:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lengthening the wings, the designers placed the lifting surfaces in a compact, closed-wing configuration. But there is another part of this design that is'

[03:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'perhaps even more interesting. A box wing is not just about aerodynamics. It\'s about the design. If we think about the wing loading of a typical airplane, we see that the wings stick out from'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the fuselage like a long cantilever beam. When a wing creates lift, it tries to curve upward. This bending load must be transmitted through the spar to the fuselage. And the longer the'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wing becomes, the more massive this structure usually has to be . But connect the wings at the ends and you suddenly have additional ways to distribute the structural load. Instead of two'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely independent cantilever wings, you start to create something closer to a closed structural frame. This can provide significant rigidity without simply weighting down each component. And there was'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'another unusual problem that the FlyNano developers had to face—the pilot. On most aircraft, the pilot makes up only a small fraction of the aircraft\'s total mass . But when your'

[04:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'empty plane weighs 70 kg and the pilot might weigh 80, 90 or even 100 kg, the pilot becomes one of the largest masses in the entire system. And this changes the whole structural'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'philosophy. The pilot must sit very close to the aircraft\'s center of gravity, as shifting such a large proportion of the total mass forward or backward would significantly affect stability. So'

[04:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'FlyNano is not just a scaled-down regular airplane. It had to be designed around the person sitting inside. And once you understand that, some of his strange'

[04:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'design decisions start to seem logical. For example, a propeller that is mounted on a pylon directly above the pilot\'s head. But the wing alone was not enough to reduce the FlyNano\'s weight to 70'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kg. The designers had to reduce weight almost everywhere. The structure was mainly made of carbon fiber composites. This provided the FlyNano with a lightweight but relatively stiff glider. There is'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'practically no regular windshield . The pilot sits in an open cockpit. And the chassis is also missing. The plane was designed as a flying boat. Instead of operating from a'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'runway , the FlyNano took off and landed directly on the water. This meant no need for wheels, tires, brakes, suspension, or landing gear cleaning mechanisms. The lower'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fuselage was effectively the hull of a boat, and the instrumentation was similarly minimal. You didn\'t have a cockpit filled with avionics , large displays, and conventional aviation systems. Anything that'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wasn\'t absolutely necessary became a candidate for deletion. This philosophy is perhaps the reason why the FlyNano has been described not as a conventional aircraft, but rather as a jet ski'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for the sky. You take it to the lake, get inside, turn up the power, accelerate across the water —and take off. There is something incredibly attractive about this simplicity. In addition, the'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electric FlyNano was surprisingly powerful. Later versions were equipped with a brushless electric motor with a power of about 32 kilowatts. That\'s about 43 horsepower.'

[06:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Given the low weight of the aircraft, this power was quite sufficient. But with enough power , it had very little energy. And that was probably his biggest limitation. The battery capacity was only'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about 1.7 kilowatt- hours. To put this into perspective: if you could somehow use the motor continuously at its full 32kW output , the battery would theoretically'

[06:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'run out in just over 3 minutes. Of course, the plane doesn\'t fly at maximum power all the time. After takeoff, the required power drops significantly. It was expected that the FlyNano would be able to provide'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'about 10–15 minutes of practical flight. Interestingly, the development of FlyNano was carried out over ten years ago. Battery technologies were far from what electric aviation enthusiasts had hoped for.'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Every extra kilowatt-hour meant adding precious kilograms of weight. And when the entire aircraft was designed for a target empty weight of 70 kg, there was simply no margin . FlyNano still flew. The'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fundamental concept worked, but turning the prototype into a commercial aircraft was another matter entirely . The company continued its efforts to develop and bring'

[07:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'FlyNano to market for several years. It even received European funding to investigate the possibility of launching mass production. The price was $39,000, which is still a modest sum compared to other'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'modern personal electric aircraft. But completing the development, setting up production, and turning such an unusual device into a sustainable business required significant additional'

[08:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'investments. And the product itself had certain limitations. You needed a suitable body of water. Conditions had to be relatively calm, the flight duration'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extremely short , the aircraft could only seat one person, and while simplicity was part of its appeal, it also severely limited its usability. Ultimately, the FlyNano disappeared, and with'

[08:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it one of the most interesting attempts to create a truly personal electric aircraft. But suppose we were to return to this idea today. Would we design FlyNano'

[08:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly the same way? Probably not. And, perhaps, the biggest mistake would be to focus on the initial figure of 70 kg. Instead, imagine allowing the plane to get a little'

[08:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heavier while still maintaining the philosophy that made the Nano interesting. Start with the wing. The compact closed wing configuration was worth preserving, as it was part of what made the aircraft so'

[09:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'distinctive. But it might be worth increasing the wingspan from 4.8 meters to about 5.5 or even 6 meters. The goal doesn\'t necessarily have to be greater speed. We can sacrifice'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speed for less wing loading, lower takeoff and landing speeds, and better efficiency. We can also remove perhaps the biggest practical limitation and make it amphibious.'

[09:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s keep the hull in the shape of a boat so it can take off from the water, but add a very lightweight , retractable landing gear . Suddenly you are no longer limited to just lakes. You can use a small airfield, a'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'grass strip, or a body of water. Next, provide the pilot with a small windshield or a lightweight cockpit light. Add a modern lightweight electronic flight display, GPS, battery monitoring, and maybe'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even basic navigation information. Today, none of these systems should weigh too much. And then we move on to the battery. The original Fly Nano had about 1.7 kWh'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '. Let\'s say a modern version would have about 4 or 5 kWh. If the aircraft can fly at 8–10 kW, then a battery of'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this capacity would potentially increase flight time to 25–30 minutes, and possibly more, depending on aerodynamics, battery reserve, and aircraft mass. Suddenly it\'s no longer a'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'toy for 10 minutes of fun by the lake. This becomes a device on which you can fully travel. Modern Mosaic rules can also give designers more'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'flexibility. Therefore, the future Fly Nano may be a little bigger, a little heavier, and significantly more functional. Perhaps the Fly Nano just came out too early . Its'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'carbon fiber structure was ready, the electric motor was ready, the unusual wing worked, but the batteries weren\'t. More than a decade later,'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
